              "cookie": [],
              "responseTime": "3034",
              "body": "{\n  \"id\": \"0722eb72323b1501978aa05ae92506ee\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    {\n      \"finish_reason\": \"stop\",\n      \"index\": 0,\n      \"message\": {\n        \"content\": \"<think>The user is asking a concise question about the difference between a large language model (LLM) and a reasoning model. I should answer in exactly two sentences as requested.\\n\\nA large language model (LLM) is trained primarily to predict the next token in a sequence based on patterns learned from vast amounts of text data, excelling at generating fluent, contextually relevant text but often struggling with multi-step logical problems. A reasoning model is specifically designed—or fine-tuned—to perform structured, step-by-step logical inference, often using techniques like chain-of-thought prompting or reinforcement learning, to tackle problems requiring deliberate deduction rather than just pattern matching.\\n\\nLet me make sure this is exactly two sentences and clearly distinguishes the two.</think>\\n\\nA large language model (LLM) is primarily trained to predict the next token in a sequence, enabling it to generate fluent, contextually relevant text based on patterns learned from vast amounts of data, but it often struggles with multi-step logical problems. A reasoning model is specifically designed or fine-tuned—often using techniques like chain-of-thought prompting or reinforcement learning—to work through problems through deliberate, step-by-step logical inference rather than relying solely on pattern matching.\",\n        \"role\": \"assistant\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"created\": 1792260211,\n  \"model\": \"MiniMax-M3\",\n  \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n  \"usage\": {\n    \"total_tokens\": 431,\n    \"total_characters\": 0,\n    \"prompt_tokens\": 194,\n    \"completion_tokens\": 237,\n    \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\n      \"cached_tokens\": 128\n    }\n  },\n  \"input_sensitive\": false,\n  \"output_sensitive\": false,\n  \"input_sensitive_